-- title:
--   Automorphism with σ(j)=j(qᵖ) moving the Gauss valuation ring
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime and $M$ a non-zero natural number with $5 \le M$ and $p \nmid M$; let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero that is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ of type $\{p\}$, and let $\zeta \in L$ be a primitive $p$-th root of unity. Let $K_1$ be an intermediate field of $L \subseteq L((q))$ equal to [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1x0FunctionFieldC ℚ M p)`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), i.e. the $L$-subfield of the Laurent series field generated by the coefficientwise images under $\mathbb{Q} \to L$ of the $\mathbb{Q}$-subfield of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ generated by the ratios of integral forms for $\Gamma_1(M) \cap \Gamma_0(p)$. Let $A$ be a discrete valuation domain with fraction field $L$, with $p$ in the maximal ideal of $A$ and with $\zeta$ in the image of $A \to L$, and let $K_1$ be an $A$-algebra compatibly with $L$. Let $j \in K_1$ be non-zero with Laurent expansion the coefficientwise image of [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157), the series $q^{-1}$ times the rational numerator power series of the modular invariant. Then there is an $L$-algebra automorphism $\sigma$ of $K_1$ with the following three properties. First, the expansion of $\sigma(j)$ is the coefficientwise image of [`ModularCurve.qExpand ℚ p ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L25), the series obtained from that of $j$ by multiplying all exponents by $p$ (i.e. $j(q^p)$). Second, for every $b \in K_1$ one has $b \in$ [`AlgebraicCurve.TwoChartIntegralModel.chartAlgFin A K₁ j`](def/AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel.html#L142) if and only if $\sigma(b)$ does, where that $A$-subalgebra consists of the elements of $K_1$ integral over $A[j]$; so $\sigma$ preserves the integral closure of $A[j]$ in $K_1$ in both directions. Third, for every valuation subring $W_0$ of $K_1$ whose elements are exactly the $f$ admitting an integral Gauss presentation — there exist power series $x, y$ over $A$ with the reduction of $y$ modulo the maximal ideal non-zero and $f \cdot \hat{y} = \hat{x}$ in $L((q))$, where $\hat{\cdot}$ denotes the image of a power series over $A$ in $L((q))$ — the preimage $\sigma^{-1}(W_0)$ (the comap of $W_0$ along $\sigma$) is different from $W_0$, while for every polynomial $P$ over $A$ with non-zero reduction both $P(j)$ and $P(j)^{-1}$ lie in $\sigma^{-1}(W_0)$.
--
--   This is the symmetry $j \mapsto j(q^p)$ of the function field of $X(\Gamma_1(M) \cap \Gamma_0(p))$ over $\mathbb{Q}(\zeta_p)$, in the form needed to compare the two branches of the semistable model at $p$: it fixes the integral $j$-chart but carries the Gauss valuation ring to a different valuation ring containing the same $P(j)^{\pm 1}$. It is used in the analysis of the non-units of the Gauss valuation subring of this function field, in the identification of the residue field attached to a Gauss presentation, and in the computation of the residue-field degree for $X_1(Mp)$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_XOneGammaZeroP_exists_algEquiv_map_j_eq_qExpand_and_chartAlgFin_iff_and_comap_ne_x1x0_gamma0.lean

